module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE, EVIDENCED ROUTE ONLY AND UNATTRIBUTED PENDING ISOLATION: in the specimen's evaluation route -- the floor's per-witness evaluation of the PR gunbc#13257 cardinality-probe family at head 74eec694d0, floor run 37207266602 -- a test-fn claim body whose comparison operand is a bare negative integer literal (the unary-minus spelling `== -1` / `== -2` rather than the corpus spelling `== 0 - 1` / `== 0 - 2`) is planned and never reaches a terminal verdict: the witness neither passes nor fails, and the floor's changed-witness gate is what eventually notices. This row does NOT claim the spelling suffices to lose a verdict everywhere: a claim body on green origin/main comparing `== -1` (dag/test/claim/bmc/bmc_onboarding_lifecycle_witness_test.dag, next_tag == -1) answers in the same corpus, so the invalid state is attributed to the specimen's route until the paired probe isolates which gate separates the two. The class is the evidenced route plus the unisolated boundary, not the spelling alone.",
    "HARM: the failure surfaces as an engine-sounding verdict (changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict) that points at the changed-witness roster rather than at the authoring spelling, so the first diagnosis is in the wrong module. Nothing in the parse, the plan, or the per-witness receipt names the literal. A claim that can never answer is worse than a red one: the red is loud where it stands, while this one is silent inside a gate whose job is to catch something else, and the author's natural spelling is the trap.",
    "RECEIPT, AND IT IS A LIVE ONE RATHER THAN A CONSTRUCTED SPECIMEN: PR gunbc#13257 at head 74eec694d0, floor run 37207266602 job 111451921277. dag/test/claim/fabric/systemd_run_transient_wait_witness_test.dag carried the one-hot cardinality probe family over `materialized_cardinality`; exactly two members -- probe_materialized_cardinality_refused and probe_materialized_cardinality_command_refused, the two whose bodies compare `== -1` and `== -2` -- were reported as identity=<probe> standing=planned-without-terminal-verdict disposition=planned_as_changed_witness outcome=failed cause=changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict. The four siblings comparing against non-negative literals all reached terminal verdicts in the same run, including probe_materialized_cardinality_11, which evaluates through the same helper whose match arms contain the same negative sentinels.",
    "THE INTERVENTION AND WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE: rewriting the two comparisons in place to `== 0 - 1` and `== 0 - 2` (commit dc666b6547 on the same branch) restored terminal verdicts for all fifteen witnesses of that entry -- ten passes and five designed fails, every one answered. The producer of that observation is named, not transcribed as a run id: the branch's own CI floor run for that commit was cancelled, so no GitHub run recorded it; the observation is the one-dispatch claim_batch verification at that commit (build + run of dag/test/claim/fabric/systemd_run_transient_wait_witness_test.dag over its test fns), and anyone re-derives it by running that entry through claim_batch at commit dc666b6547 and reading the per-witness verdicts. That is a real paired observation in one entry, but it does NOT isolate the spelling as the whole boundary, and this row refuses to overclaim it: the census command below returns further occurrences of the unary spelling across dag/ and src/v2/ at filing, including claim modules that reach terminal verdicts on green main -- dag/test/claim/bmc/bmc_onboarding_lifecycle_witness_test.dag compares `next_tag(p: FabricJoined) == -1` inside a Bool claim body and answers. So the class is narrower than the spelling: some gate -- engine version, module context, or evaluator route -- lets the same spelling answer elsewhere while refusing it in the specimen. The boundary is real but unisolated, and the `0 - N` spelling is the standing safe one the corpus already uses everywhere it must answer.",
    "POPULATION, BY INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN BY ASSERTION: the census at filing grain is the reproduction command, not a number copied into this row -- `git grep -E \"(==|=>) -[0-9]\"` over dag/ and src/v2/ -- per DESIGN section 6: a measurement is cited by naming the producer that re-derives it. At filing the command's occurrences included the specimen's two, the only ones observed losing a verdict. A future run of the same command whose result reclassifies any occurrence into this row's standing is how the population grows.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mechanically preventable, by the required floor's own missing-terminal-verdict gate. The floor's changed-witness gate (changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict) mechanically contains the invalid state: a witness that never answers cannot carry a floor phase to FloorClean -- the state is detected, enrolled, and refused at the required-floor adjudication, which is a wall in the rung sense even though nothing in the parse, normalize, resolve, or claim-body lowering refuses the SPELLING. What the gate does not do is name the spelling: it files the failure under the changed-witness roster, so the first diagnosis lands in the wrong module, and the corpus convention (`0 - N` copied from neighbouring claim bodies) is what keeps authors out of the trap. A convention is not a wall and this rung does not rest on it; it rests on the floor gate that refuses the phase.",
    "CEILING, SPLIT BECAUSE THE TWO WALLS DO NOT REACH THE SAME HEIGHT AND NEITHER RETIRES THE OTHER: (a) canonicalising the unary-minus integer literal at the literal-to-term boundary -- lowering `== -N` to the term `== 0 - N` lowers to -- leaves no accepted program that evaluates the spelling differently, so the invalid state becomes unconstructible and the ceiling for THAT wall is structurally impossible; (b) refusing the spelling at declaration with a typed located diagnostic naming the span leaves the spelling in the language but guarantees it never evaluates -- the compiler refuses, the state is caught before it can answer or hang, and the ceiling for THAT wall is structurally guaranteed, not structurally impossible, because the rejected source still parses. The row is retired only when the boundary is isolated and at least one wall is placed on the isolated boundary; a wall placed on an unisolated boundary would be sized to a guess, so the isolation experiment precedes either.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: the isolation experiment first -- one module, one Int helper returning a negative sentinel, two test fns differing in exactly one term (`== -1` versus `== 0 - 1`), run under the floor's own pinned engine rather than a session-built one, with the verdict-less arm's lowering trace captured. Then the wall: the claim-body lowering either lowers a unary-minus integer literal to the term `0 - N` lowers to, or refuses it at declaration with a typed located diagnostic naming the span, executed on that paired probe -- the refused arm must lose its verdict NO MORE (it must be refused, not planned), and the `0 - N` arm must evaluate unchanged. Owner unassigned at filing. This row is NOT retired by the corpus convention, by a session-local workaround, or by the specimen's own fix.",
    "DISPOSITION, PENDING AT A NAMED HEAD RATHER THAN CLAIMED OF MAIN: filed from gunbc#13257, the debt-paydown trial's typed-argv cutover, whose floor run is the receipt. #13257 carried the corpus `0 - N` spelling as its fix and deliberately does NOT build the lowering wall -- that is this row's trigger, not that PR's scope. That PR has since restructured the specimen family: the five designed-fail one-hot probes were rewritten as TRUE-RETURNING negative controls -- each control asserts its own discrimination as a conjunction (cardinality != 0 && != 1 && != 10, the refused arm is not produced), so the absence claims go red rather than silent and no designed-false claim remains in the tree. The restructured family and its verdicts live on the #13257 branch at head f4c164a135 (side-chat-approved, review 5427676189; CI fully green, run 37693433097), queued for merge -- until it lands, this row's restructured-standing claim is a claim about that named head, not about origin/main's tree, and the row's receipt above remains the live historical observation. At that head the entry carries 17 test fns (14 witnesses plus 3 true-returning controls) and the floor receipt for that head's run (37693433097) shows no planned-without-terminal-verdict identity in the entry and nothing enrolled expected-red. This row is NOT retired by the restructure, by the corpus convention, or by the specimen's own fix.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
